n a separate module, and Lean's comparator is the tool for checking that the module proves exactly what the statement file says.\n\n  * Universal irreducible tensor squares for symmetric groups: statement, proof. Comparator run of 2026-10-26: accepted (log). Accepted on a retry that took about 4 hours; the first run hit the 2-hour limit.\n  * Saxl's conjecture: statement, proof. Comparator run of 2026-10-26: accepted (log).\n\nThe comparator runs are described, with what they show and what they do not, in docs/LEAN_CHECKS.md.\n\nNobody has checked whether the Lean statements say what the paper claims.\n\n## Family 205: Saxl’s conjecture and universal tensor squares\n\nProves Saxl's conjecture: the tensor square of every staircase representation contains every irreducible complex representation of the corresponding symmetric group. More generally, every S_{n} with $n\\notin\\{2,4,9\\}$ has an irreducible representation whose tensor square contains all irreducibles.\n\nOther papers in this family:\n\n  * Universal Tensor Squares for Symmetric Groups · September 24, 2026\n\nManuscripts, family summaries and abstracts: OpenAI, openai/math (Apache 2.0). Notes and revisions: contributors to mathcommunity/oai-math-revised.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) The S3\\documentclass[12pt]{minimal} \\usepackage{amsmath} \\usepackage{wasysym} \\usepackage{amsfonts} \\usepackage{amssymb} \\usepackage{amsbsy} \\usepackage{mathrsfs} \\usepackage{upgreek} \\setlength{\\oddsidemargin}{-69pt} \\begin{document}$$S_3$$\\end{document}-symmetric tridiagonal algebra (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/392838381_The_S3documentclass12ptminimal_usepackageamsmath_usepackagewasysym_usepackageamsfonts_usepackageamssymb_usepackageamsbsy_usepackagemathrsfs_usepackageupgreek_setlengthoddsidemargin-69pt_begindocumentS)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; 3 The tridiagonal algebra ... The relati